# Nav — the left icon rail. A `ui` facet: pure presentation that snaps into the
# `nav` socket of a wireframe. No data, no durable state — just icons + links.
#
# EVERY ROW USED TO POINT AT "/" AND THAT WAS A WORKAROUND, NOT A DESIGN. A link
# to a route no view serves is a build failure, and until a `ui`/`data` facet
# could declare routed `view`s there was nowhere in a layered build for
# /profile/:handle to be served from — so the rail pointed four rows at the one
# route that existed. `data/feed.fct` serves /profile/:handle now, so Profile
# points at the signed-in actor's own page. The destination is the path-template
# form, which is checked against the app's routes at build time: this row is a
# dead link at compile time or it is not a dead link at all.
#
# Explore and Notifications still point at "/" because nothing in this
# composition serves them yet, and they are left honest rather than pointed at
# invented routes — a rail row that goes home is a row that has not been built,
# and a rail row that 404s is a bug.
# The four marks below are `ui/icon.fct`'s. A `ui` facet has no `css:` block of
# its own — it is content snapped into a wireframe socket — so the set has to
# arrive by import or the rail draws four blank squares.
import "icon.fct"
ui Nav in nav:
    content:
        text "𝕏 · F33D3R"
        row:
            icon "home"
            link "Home" -> "/"
        row:
            icon "search"
            link "Explore" -> "/"
        row:
            icon "bell"
            link "Notifications" -> "/"
        row:
            icon "user"
            link "Profile" -> "/profile/{actor}"
        text "signed in as {actor}"
        button "log out" -> logout
